\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Detector performance against human ground truth}
\Cref{tab:perclass} reports \rg{} against the $200$ human labels. The headline numbers are poor---accuracy $0.30$, macro-F1 $0.19$, $\kappa=0.16$---and miss the pre-registered target, but the aggregate hides two sharply opposite behaviours. On fabrication (V1), \rg{} is \emph{high-precision, low-recall} ($0.90$/$0.09$): of $98$ human-labelled fabrications it recovers only $9$ (classifying the rest as \textsc{none}, \textsc{mixed}, or V4), but when it does fire V1 it is almost always right. On under-specification (V4) it is the reverse ($0.48$/$0.92$): it recovers $23$ of $25$ but over-triggers. This asymmetry, not a uniform failure, is the substantive finding.

